 genomes from  like mammoths and mastodons that have  been extinct for some time and then this  type of sequencing that we call  next-generation sequencing was invented  next-generation sequencing allows us to  extract every bit of DNA that is  preserved within a bone or a hair or  fragment of an organism that used to be  alive and sequence all of it and in this  way we can begin to piece together the  entire genome of an extinct animal like  a mammoth or in the and earth all so the  genetic information that we extract from  these fossils can tell us a lot about  the populations that used to be alive we  can learn when they were growing when  they were declining the climate change  that is happening today is not the first  time that the earth has experienced  rapid warming and hopefully with these  data we can make more informed decisions  about how to use the limited resources  we have today to protect living and  endangered species in the face of  present-day climate change  you
 

